Verdict: MAJOR  
Retrieval adequacy: adequate

Strengths
- The plan correctly recognizes the current schema gap: annual-disclosure contracts need row-level provenance fields that do not exist in the current sanction-point `CostDataPoint` surface.
- The plan keeps automation implementation itself out of scope and frames duplicate/conflict handling as contract-only work.

Findings by severity

Critical
- The proposed implementation surface is mis-grounded in current repo files. `src/worldenergydata/cost/data_collection/calibration_schema.py` and `src/worldenergydata/cost/data_collection/public_dataset.py` are sanction-point artifacts built around `CostDataPoint(project_name, year_sanction, source, confidence, ...)`, not annual disclosure records with `fiscal_year`, `scope`, `normalized_metric_name`, `source_title`, `source_url`, `page_reference`, and `quoted_text`. The plan’s own pseudocode depends on fields that do not exist on the named target model. This should not pass plan review unless it either targets the disclosure-layer files from `#334` or explicitly creates disclosure-specific contract surfaces.
- The plan steals parent/sibling work. Parent `#334` already includes “minimum row-level provenance and citation rules” in the disclosure schema and seed dataset. This child plan redefines core row-level provenance inside current sanction-dataset files instead of building on the disclosure-layer boundary. That is overlap with `#334`, not a bounded child follow-up.
- The plan’s file list and acceptance criteria are internally inconsistent with its own scope. It says “annual disclosures” and “future ingest contract,” but its only listed data file change is `public_dataset.py`, which is the existing sanctioned-project dataset. No disclosure-specific schema/dataset/test file is named, so there is no concrete surface where annual-disclosure ingest contracts would live.

High
- `tests/unit/cost/test_dataset_size.py` is the wrong test surface for “dataset-level provenance and duplicate/conflict guardrails.” That file currently enforces size, regional coverage, year span, HPHT presence, and confidence distribution for the sanction dataset. Adding annual-disclosure duplicate/conflict semantics there would couple unrelated datasets and create false failures as the sanction corpus evolves.
- “Modify `public_dataset.py` to align representative source rows/docs with stricter provenance contract if needed” is scope creep into broad backfill. The current dataset has a single freeform `source` string across many records. Converting even representative rows to a row-level structured citation contract inside the sanction dataset is not a narrow annual-disclosure contract task.
- The duplicate key in pseudocode uses `fiscal_year`, `scope`, and `normalized_metric_name`, but the plan does not identify any schema object or fixture source that will supply those fields. Without the disclosure schema from `#334`, duplicate/conflict tests are synthetic-only and disconnected from the intended repo surface.

Medium
- The source-priority hierarchy is underspecified. The plan says priority must be “explicit and ordered,” but no acceptance criterion says how that ordering affects duplicate/conflict classification, invalidation, or tie-breaking. That makes the hierarchy easy to add as an enum and impossible to validate meaningfully.
- The plan exports new primitives through `src/worldenergydata/cost/data_collection/__init__.py`, but it never defines whether those primitives are disclosure-specific or generic. If they are generic, they risk contaminating the sanction-point boundary; if disclosure-specific, the missing disclosure module/file list becomes a blocker.
- “Existing sanction-dataset tests continue to pass unchanged” conflicts with the proposed willingness to modify sanction dataset rows/docs. If the sanction schema remains unchanged, disclosure citation requirements cannot be enforced there; if the sanction schema changes, unchanged tests are not a meaningful acceptance signal.

Low
- The plan does not specify whether `page_reference` must support non-page locators used in web-native disclosures, appendices, or tables. That omission may force a brittle validator.
- “absolute source_url” is too vague without scheme restrictions. `mailto:` and other absolute URIs are technically absolute but not valid source documents.

Missing tests
- A positive-path test for a valid disclosure row satisfying the full citation contract.
- A test proving sanction-point `CostDataPoint` records remain valid and unaffected if this issue is truly bounded away from sanction-schema redesign.
- A test defining how source-priority ordering changes behavior when two rows with the same business key conflict.
- A test covering duplicate classification when non-key metadata differs but value/citation payload is identical.
- A test for invalid partition reasons or error payload shape from `ingest_contract`, not just bucket membership.

Scope creep concerns
- Reworking `public_dataset.py` provenance beyond documentation/examples is parent-level or separate migration work.
- Putting annual-disclosure ingest rules into `calibration_schema.py` mixes disclosure-layer concerns into the sanction-point calibration model.
- Using `test_dataset_size.py` for disclosure guardrails expands this child issue into sanction-dataset governance.

Weakest assumption
- That annual-disclosure citation and ingest contracts can be cleanly defined inside the current sanction-point `cost.data_collection` files without first landing or targeting the disclosure-layer files introduced by `#334`.

Most likely implementation failure mode
- The implementer adds disclosure-only validators and enums into `calibration_schema.py`, then either has to retrofit `CostDataPoint`/`public_dataset.py` to satisfy them or creates parallel unused primitives that are not exercised by any real disclosure dataset.

Most likely test gap
- Tests pass against fabricated dicts for `ingest_contract`, but no real disclosure-layer schema/dataset exists in the named files, so the contract is unproven against the actual intended repo surface.

Future issues suggested
- A narrow migration issue, if desired later, to upgrade the legacy sanction dataset from freeform `source` strings to structured row-level citations.
- A separate restatement/versioning issue if annual disclosures will eventually need prior-year restatement handling.
- A follow-up clarifying source-priority semantics beyond ordering alone: tie-breaking, override rules, and conflict adjudication.

Review confidence
- High
